## Milestone

M4 (author, 2026-09-29: "Zotero storage smells like M4 work"). Do not start
before the milestone opens.

## Context

Tracker. Author decision (2026-09-29, Imagine conversation): the JETP
observatory should not maintain its own document management. Zotero offers a
stable data model and a plugin ecosystem, and the author cites these documents
from Zotero anyway (EDM rule), so any other store would make two catalogues of
the same items. A due-diligence check of Python alternatives (papis, DataLad /
git-annex, Paperless-ngx, Mayan EDMS, Calibre) found none better for this role.

Today step D1 is a home-made document manager: `data/jetp/documents.csv`
(392 rows), `snapshots.csv`, and the content-addressed object store
`data/jetp/documents/objects/<sha256>` under DVC (padme remote, about 282 MB).

## Division of labour

- Zotero (a dedicated group library, separate from the author's personal
  library, cloud-synced for distribution) holds document identity (title, publisher, date,
  language, type, URL), files (one attachment per byte version), edition and
  translation links, reading, annotation and citation.
- The repo keeps what is research data, not document management:
  `retrievals.csv`, `publisher-link-checks.csv`, `web-archive-captures.csv`,
  `decisions.md`, and steps D2-D4.

## Design constraints

1. The existing `document_id` goes into each Zotero item's Extra field, so the
   D2-D4 keys stay unchanged.
2. The pipeline never reads live Zotero. It reads a committed export of the
   group library (Web API, or a Better BibTeX auto-export), pinned like any
   other input.
3. Every locator records the hash of the file it read. A check flags an
   attachment whose bytes changed. A new publisher version becomes a new
   attachment on the same item.
4. DVC shrinks to a hash-keyed cache filled from Zotero, plus the frozen
   evidence for each release. It stops being a second system of record.
5. The group is PublicClosed: an open public group allows no file sharing,
   while a closed-membership public group shares metadata with everyone and
   attachments with members only (author, 2026-09-29; details in 1511), so
   the files stay
   non-redistributed, as in `docs/jetp-storage.md` ("not served wholesale").
   Public access to the documents themselves already goes through the Web
   Archive snapshots (`web-archive-captures.csv`).

## Children

- 1511: pilot on Viet Nam (58 documents, the smallest country).
- Later children are filed after the pilot's findings: the remaining
  countries, switching the harvester (`corpus_harvest_ledger.py`) to write
  through the Web API (reuse the `zotero-import` / `index-source` machinery),
  and retiring `documents.csv` as a hand-edited table.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every JETP document lives in the group library, with its `document_id`
      in Extra.
- [ ] The pipeline builds D1-D4 from the committed export, with no read of
      live Zotero.
- [ ] The observatory and the D2-D4 outputs are byte-identical before and
      after the migration.
- [ ] Integration review done (`/roar` step 8).
